/*
We have tools/orders. For each tool i:
- name
- a[i]: time to create without support
- support tool name p[i] (or maybe absent / "-" / "0")
- b[i]: time to create with that support

If the support tool is available before starting tool i, we may craft i in b[i] days instead of a[i].
Each tool is crafted exactly once, sequentially, minimizing total completion time of all orders.

Observation:
For any dependency edge parent -> child (child can be faster if parent already built), this creates a precedence relation only if we want the discount.
The dependency graph is functional (each node names at most one support), but many nodes may point to same support.
Cycles matter:
- In a cycle, no node can get support from within the cycle unless some cycle node is built first without that support.
- More generally, choose for each node either normal cost a[i], or discounted cost b[i] if its support chosen earlier.
This is equivalent to finding an order minimizing total sum.

Standard solution:
Let delta[i] = a[i] - b[i] (benefit from having support first).

For an acyclic component (tree edges from support to dependent), optimal ordering:
build supports before descendants whenever beneficial; since b[i] <=? not guaranteed, but if delta<=0 no need to wait.
We can process DAG by indegrees after contracting cycles:
For an edge u -> v (u is support of v), if delta[v] > 0, it's beneficial to place u before v.
If delta[v] <= 0, v should be done before u if possible, because waiting for support doesn't help.
This is the classic "sort by sign" on dependency graph:
DFS/postorder:
- if delta[v] > 0: output after processing children? Actually known from Codeforces problem "B. ..." where maximize sum with propagation.
But here dependency is simple and cost reduction local.

A cleaner DP on functional graph:
Total cost = sum a[i] - sum delta[i] over nodes whose support is built earlier.
Thus maximize total gained delta over satisfied edges.
In each non-cycle node, we can always satisfy its edge iff along path to root/cycle we order ancestors appropriately. Since satisfying a node's edge doesn't conflict with satisfying descendants' positive edges: place parent before child. For negative/nonpositive delta, we simply don't satisfy.
So all positive-delta tree nodes can be satisfied simultaneously by topological order from roots/cycle outward, provided cycle handling.

For a directed cycle, we can satisfy all positive-delta edges except at least one edge must be unsatisfied (the first built node in the cycle cannot have its predecessor before it).
If we choose node x as first in cycle, then edge pred(x)->x is unsatisfied; every other positive edge can be satisfied by following cycle order.
Additionally, nodes with delta<=0 naturally needn't be satisfied, and can be placed earlier.

Hence for each cycle:
gain from cycle = sum(max(delta[i],0)) - penalty
where penalty = min over edges forced unsatisfied:
   if delta[x] > 0 and edge pred(x)->x would otherwise contribute delta[x], losing it costs delta[x];
   if delta[x] <= 0, losing it costs 0.
So penalty = min_i max(delta[i],0).
Thus cycle contributes all positive deltas except the smallest positive one (or 0 if any nonpositive exists).

Therefore minimal total time:
sum a[i] - (sum positive deltas on non-cycle nodes) - (for each cycle, sum positive deltas in cycle - min positive_or_zero)

Need parse input. Since statement excerpt incomplete, assume:
n
name a support b
...
Support might be "0" if none / no support. We'll treat unknown support names as none unless mapped later? Since names all from orders likely.
Build name->id first.

Then detect cycles in functional graph support[i].
If support missing/self? self forms cycle length1.
For nodes with no valid support, parent=-1.

Answer long long.
*/

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    if (!(cin >> n)) return 0;

    vector<string> name(n), supName(n);
    vector<long long> a(n), b(n);
    unordered_map<string,int> id;
    id.reserve(n * 2);

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        cin >> name[i] >> a[i] >> supName[i] >> b[i];
        id[name[i]] = i;
    }

    vector<int> parent(n, -1);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        auto it = id.find(supName[i]);
        if (it != id.end()) parent[i] = it->second;
        else parent[i] = -1;
    }

    vector<long long> delta(n);
    long long ans = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        ans += a[i];
        delta[i] = a[i] - b[i];
    }

    // Detect nodes in cycles of functional graph.
    vector<int> state(n, 0); // 0 unvisited, 1 visiting, 2 done
    vector<int> inCycle(n, 0);

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        if (state[i]) continue;
        int u = i;
        unordered_map<int,int> pos;
        while (u != -1 && state[u] == 0 && !pos.count(u)) {
            pos[u] = (int)pos.size();
            u = parent[u];
        }
        if (u != -1 && state[u] == 0 && pos.count(u)) {
            int v = u;
            do {
                inCycle[v] = 1;
                v = parent[v];
            } while (v != u);
        }
        u = i;
        while (u != -1 && state[u] == 0) {
            state[u] = 2;
            u = parent[u];
        }
        for (auto &kv : pos) state[kv.first] = 2;
    }

    // Add gains from non-cycle nodes with positive delta.
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        if (!inCycle[i] && delta[i] > 0) ans -= delta[i];
    }

    // Process each cycle once.
    vector<int> vis(n, 0);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        if (!inCycle[i] || vis[i]) continue;
        long long sumPos = 0;
        long long mnLoss = (long long)4e18;
        int u = i;
        do {
            vis[u] = 1;
            long long loss = max(0LL, delta[u]);
            sumPos += loss;
            mnLoss = min(mnLoss, loss);
            u = parent[u];
        } while (u != i);
        ans -= (sumPos - mnLoss);
    }

    cout << ans << '\n';
    return 0;
}

